1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Welcome back! Now that we've got all our requirements in place,

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,500
it's time to set up the database. This is where VentureX will

3
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:12,000
store all its data — customer records, transactions, inventory,

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,000
everything. Let's get into it.

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:19,500
I'm going to show you three ways to set this up. First, the command line —

6
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:23,000
this is the most direct method and my personal favorite.

7
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:26,500
Open your terminal and log into MySQL as the root user.

8
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:31,000
You'll be prompted for your root password. Type it in —

9
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:34,500
you won't see the characters as you type, that's normal for security.

10
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:38,000
Hit Enter, and if your password is correct, you'll see the MySQL monitor prompt.

11
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,500
Alright, we're in. Now let's create the database.

12
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:46,000
I'm going to type this command exactly.

13
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:50,500
VENTUREX_ERP — that's the name of our database.

14
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:54,000
The semicolon at the end is important, don't forget it.

15
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:57,500
You should see 'Query OK' which means the database was created successfully.

16
00:00:57,500 --> 00:01:01,000
Let's verify it exists.

17
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:05,500
There it is — VENTUREX_ERP shows up in the list. Perfect.

18
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:09,500
Next, we need to create a dedicated user for this database.

19
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:13,000
You never want to use root for your application —

20
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:17,000
that's a security best practice. Let's create a user with limited privileges.

21
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:21,500
I'm creating a user called VENTUREX_ERP_user that can only connect

22
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:25,000
from localhost — that's the 127.0.0.1 part.

23
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:28,500
And I'm setting a strong password. Now, replace 'YourSecurePassword123!'

24
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:32,000
with something unique and strong.

25
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:36,000
Don't use the same password I'm using here.

26
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,500
Now we need to give this user permission to actually work with our database.

27
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:44,000
We'll grant all privileges on the VENTUREX_ERP database

28
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,500
to our new user.

29
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:52,000
This gives our user full access to everything inside the VENTUREX_ERP database,

30
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,500
but nothing outside of it. That's exactly what we want.

31
00:01:55,500 --> 00:01:59,000
Let's flush the privileges to make sure MySQL picks up the changes.

32
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:03,500
Now, if the command line feels a bit intimidating, here's the GUI approach

33
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:07,000
using phpMyAdmin. This comes bundled with tools like XAMPP and WAMP,

34
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:10,500
so you might already have it.

35
00:02:11,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Log in with your MySQL credentials. Once you're in,

36
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,500
click on the 'Databases' tab at the top.

37
00:02:19,500 --> 00:02:23,000
Type your database name — VENTUREX_ERP — and hit Create.

38
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:26,500
One click, done. For creating a user, go to the 'User accounts' tab,

39
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:30,000
click 'Add user account', fill in the username, host, and password,

40
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:34,000
then scroll down and check 'Grant all privileges' for the VENTUREX_ERP database.

41
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:38,500
And for those of you who prefer a desktop application,

42
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:42,000
MySQL Workbench is another great option. It's free and available

43
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,000
for Windows, Mac, and Linux.

44
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:50,500
Connect to your MySQL server, then open a new query tab.

45
00:02:50,500 --> 00:02:54,000
You can paste the same SQL commands we ran in the terminal —

46
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:57,500
the CREATE DATABASE, CREATE USER, and GRANT PRIVILEGES statements.

47
00:02:57,500 --> 00:03:01,000
Execute them one by one, and you're set.

48
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:05,500
Let's verify everything is working. I'll log in with the new user credentials

49
00:03:05,500 --> 00:03:09,000
to make sure the user was created correctly.

50
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:13,500
Now let's check that we can see our database.

51
00:03:14,500 --> 00:03:18,000
And there it is — VENTUREX_ERP is visible to our new user.

52
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:21,500
That confirms the privileges were granted correctly.

53
00:03:21,500 --> 00:03:25,000
Our database is ready to go.

54
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:29,500
That's it! Your database is set up and ready.

55
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:33,000
In the next video, we'll configure the VentureX environment file

56
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:36,500
and connect it to this database. Almost there —

57
00:03:36,500 --> 00:03:40,000
see you in the next one!
